const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"LR_E3A_Q001","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":false,"question_text":"Rama, Bhima and Bond have to take two articles each from the available six articles – Gun, Bow, Arrow, Mace, Sword and Dagger. Bhima will not take the Gun or the Bow, and Bond will not take any of the Sword, the Bow or the Dagger. If one of the three persons takes the Sword and the Mace, then what is the other item selected by the person who selects the Gun?","options":{"A":"Bow","B":"Arrow","C":"Dagger","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Arrow","solution_text":"Bond will not take a Sword or a Bow or Dagger. Bhima will not take a Gun or a Bow. Bow must be taken by Rama. Sword and Mace can be taken by Bhima only and Rama has to take the Dagger, as Bond will not take the Dagger. Gun and Arrow are taken by Bond.","entity_count":3,"variables":2,"constraints":2,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"low","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination"],"question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q002","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":false,"question_text":"Four students – Sagar, Swaroop, Sachin and Suman – went to four different cities – Mumbai, Vijayawada, Hyderabad and Nagpur – to take 4 different tests – XAT, JMET, CET, CAT. The following data is also known. 1. Suman did not take JMET. 2. Swaroop did not go to Hyderabad and he did not take CET and XAT. 3. CET was conducted in Nagpur. 4. Sagar did not go to Nagpur and he did not take CAT. 5. Sachin had gone to Mumbai. If Sagar did not take XAT, then which of the following is the correct combination of the city visited and the test written by Swaroop?","options":{"A":"Vijayawada and JMET","B":"Nagpur and JMET","C":"Hyderabad and CAT","D":"Vijayawada and CAT"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Vijayawada and CAT","solution_text":"From 1, we know that Suman did not write JMET. From 2 and 3, we know that Swaroop did not go to Hyderabad or Nagpur (as he did not write CET which was conducted in Nagpur). From 5, we know that Sachin went to Mumbai which means Swaroop did not go to Mumbai, which implies that she must have gone to Vijayawada and written either JMET or CAT. From 4, we know that Sagar did not write CAT or CET (as he did not go to Nagpur) and from the last statement we also know that Sagar did not write XAT, which means that he must have written JMET. This implies that Swaroop wrote CAT. Swaroop goes to Vijayawada and writes CAT.","entity_count":4,"variables":3,"constraints":5,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination"],"question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q003","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":false,"question_text":"Each of the five trains - GT Express, AP Express, Rajdhani Express, Goa Express and Bangalore Express travel to a different city among Delhi, Goa, Chennai, Bangalore and Hyderabad, not necessarily in the same order. No two trains travel to the same city and no two cities can be reached by the same train. GT Express goes to neither Delhi nor Chennai. Neither AP Express nor Goa Express goes to Hyderabad. Bangalore can be reached by either Goa Express or AP Express. Rajdhani Express travels to Chennai. Either Goa Express goes to Hyderabad or the Bangalore Express goes to Delhi. Which one of the following statements would help in completing the arrangement?","options":{"A":"Either Chennai can be reached by Rajdhani Express or Goa can be reached by AP Express.","B":"Only if Bangalore can be reached by Bangalore Express, then Goa can be reached by Goa Express.","C":"If Goa Express goes to Bangalore, then AP Express goes to Goa.","D":"If Bangalore can be reached by AP Express, then Delhi can be reached by Bangalore Express."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Only if Bangalore can be reached by Bangalore Express, then Goa can be reached by Goa Express.","solution_text":"Forming a grid between the trains and the cities, we get the following arrangement: (i) GTXP ≠ Delhi, Chennai (ii) Hyd ≠ APXP, GoaXP (iii) Bangalore = GoaXP or APXP, Bangalore ≠ RajXP, GTXP, BangaloreXP (iv) RajXP = Chennai (v) Either GoaXP = Hyd or BangaloreXP = Delhi. As GoaXP ≠ Hyd, BangaloreXP = Delhi, GTXP = Hyd. (A) Either p or q: Implications: p → q and q → p. But p is true (i.e. Chennai is visited by RajXP), hence q may or may not be true (i.e. Goa may or may not be visited by APXP). Hence, (A) will not help in completing the arrangement. (B) Only if p, then q: Implications: q → p; p → q. Here, Bangalore is not visited by the BangaloreXP (p), which implies that Goa is not visited by the GoaXP (p → q). Hence, this statement helps in completing the arrangement. (C) If p, then q: Implications: p → q; q → p. Both p and q are undecided here, as it is not known whether GoaXP visits Bangalore or not, and APXP visits Goa or not. (D) If p, then q: Here, Delhi is visited by BangaloreXP, means that q is true, but can’t say whether p is true or not (since p → q). Hence, it is only (B) which helps in completing the arrangement.","entity_count":5,"variables":2,"constraints":5,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["conditional_logic","constraint_elimination"],"question_operation":"identify_completing_statement","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180},{"set_id":"LR_E3A_S4_6","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Six persons – P, Q, R, S, T and U carry an umbrella and a bag of six different colours while going to school. The colours of each of the umbrellas and each of the bags are one among red, yellow, green, blue, pink and black. None among them carries an umbrella and a bag of the same colour. Further the following information is known. (i) S carries a blue coloured umbrella but not a black coloured bag. (ii) The person who carries a pink coloured umbrella carries a green coloured bag. (iii) P carries a red coloured bag but not a yellow coloured umbrella. (iv) R carries a black coloured umbrella and T carries a yellow coloured bag. (v) Q does not carry a black coloured bag.","setup_solution":"The given information is represented in the following table. Name Umbrella Bag: P ≠ Yellow, Red; Q ≠ black; R Black ≠ black; S Blue ≠ black; T Yellow; U. From the above table it is clear that U carries a black coloured bag. From (ii) and the above table, S does not carry a blue, black, red, green or yellow coloured bag. S carries a pink coloured bag. From (ii) the pink coloured umbrella and green coloured bag are carried by Q. R carries a blue coloured umbrella. As P is not carrying a yellow coloured umbrella, P is carrying the green coloured umbrella. T cannot carry a yellow coloured umbrella. U is carrying a yellow coloured umbrella. T is carrying the red coloured umbrella. The final table: P Green Red; Q Pink Green; R Black Blue; S Blue Pink; T Red Yellow; U Yellow Black.","entity_count":6,"variables":2,"constraints":5,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3A_Q004","question_text":"Who carries a red coloured umbrella?","options":{"A":"T","B":"U","C":"Q","D":"Data inadequate"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"T","solution_text":"T is carrying a red coloured umbrella.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q005","question_text":"Which colour bag is carried by the person who carries a yellow coloured umbrella?","options":{"A":"Blue","B":"Black","C":"Pink","D":"Either (A) or (C)"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Black","solution_text":"U is carrying a yellow coloured umbrella and the colour of his bag is black.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q006","question_text":"Which of the following is the correct combination of the person colour of umbrella and bag he/she carries respectively?","options":{"A":"Q – red – blue","B":"Q – red – black","C":"U – red – black","D":"T – red – yellow"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"T – red – yellow","solution_text":"T – red, yellow is the correct combination.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3A_S7_9","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Each the five friends-Dweep, Manyata, Jagat, Poulami and Hemant, has at least one of the following items – pen, pencil, bag, ruler, calculator and eraser. Hemant has pen, pencil and calculator only. Jagat has calculator and pen only. Dweep has eraser and bag only. Poulami has eraser, bag and pencil only. Manyata has only one item. Each item is with at least one person.","setup_solution":"Name Item: Hemant Pen, Pencil, Calculator; Jagat Calculator, Pen; Dweep Eraser, Bag; Poulami Eraser, Bag, Pencil. As none among Hemant, Jagat, Dweep and Poulami has a ruler, Manyata has ruler.","entity_count":5,"variables":2,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"low","solving_methodologies":["set_membership"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3A_Q007","question_text":"Who has ruler?","options":{"A":"Hemant","B":"Poulami","C":"Manyata","D":"Data inadequate"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Manyata","solution_text":"Manyata has a ruler.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q008","question_text":"Which of the following gives the complete list of the people who have pencils?","options":{"A":"Hemant","B":"Poulami","C":"Hemant and Poulami","D":"Hemant, Dweep and Poulami"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Hemant and Poulami","solution_text":"Hemant and Poulami have pencils.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q009","question_text":"Which of the items is there with more than two friends?","options":{"A":"Pencil","B":"Calculator","C":"Bag","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"None of these","solution_text":"None of the items is present with more than two people.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3A_S10_12","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following data. Nine games Cricket, Football, Hockey, Badminton, Chess, Tennis, Squash, Table-Tennis and Basketball are played by three players Kallis, Ronaldo and Sampras. Each player plays at least two games and each game is played by exactly one player. The number of games played by each player is different. (i) Neither Ronaldo nor Sampras plays Cricket. (ii) Neither Kallis nor Sampras plays Football. (iii) Neither Kallis nor Ronaldo plays the maximum number of games; neither Ronaldo nor Sampras plays the minimum number of games. (iv) Ronaldo plays one game among Hockey, Badminton and Chess. (v) Sampras plays one game between Hockey and Badminton, Chess and Squash, Basketball and Table-Tennis. (vi) Kallis plays Badminton and Ronaldo does not play Table-Tennis.","setup_solution":"It is given that three players Kallis, Ronaldo and Sampras play some games among the nine given games. Each player plays at least two games and the number of games played by each player is different. By this we can conclude that these players play 2 games, 3 games and 4 games. From (i), we get Kallis plays Cricket. From (ii), we get Ronaldo plays Football. From (iii), we get Sampras plays four games and Kallis plays two games. As the number of games played by each player is different, Ronaldo plays three games. From (v), we get Sampras definitely plays Tennis. From (vi), we get Kallis plays Badminton i.e., Kallis plays only two games, Cricket and Badminton. From (vi) and (v), we get Sampras plays Hockey and Ronaldo plays Chess. As Sampras does not play Chess, he plays Squash. Ronaldo does not play Table-Tennis means he plays Basketball and Sampras plays Table-Tennis. Kallis: Cricket, Badminton. Ronaldo: Football, Chess, Basketball. Sampras: Hockey, Tennis, Squash, Table-Tennis.","entity_count":3,"variables":2,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3A_Q010","question_text":"Which games does Sampras play?","options":{"A":"Hockey, Chess and Squash.","B":"Football, Chess and Basketball.","C":"Hockey, Tennis, Squash and Table-Tennis.","D":"Tennis, Squash, Football and Chess."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Hockey, Tennis, Squash and Table-Tennis.","solution_text":"Sampras plays Hockey, Tennis, Squash and Table-Tennis.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q011","question_text":"Which games does Ronaldo play?","options":{"A":"Football, Chess, Basketball and Squash.","B":"Football, Tennis and Chess.","C":"Football, Hockey, Chess and Squash.","D":"Football, Chess and Basketball."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Football, Chess and Basketball.","solution_text":"Ronaldo plays Football, Chess and Basketball.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q012","question_text":"Which among the following is definitely true?","options":{"A":"Kallis plays three games.","B":"Ronaldo does not play Chess and Basketball.","C":"Sampras does not play Cricket, Football and Chess.","D":"Kallis plays Cricket, Badminton and Squash."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Sampras does not play Cricket, Football and Chess.","solution_text":"Sampras does not play Cricket, Football and Chess is the only statement that is true.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3A_S13_15","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Four students Rashmi, Rukmini, Rajni and Rosy participated in the 100 m race of the annual sports meet and finished in the first four positions, not necessarily in that order. Each of these students was wearing a different coloured jersey and each jersey had one different number written over it, from among the numbers 1, 2, 3 and 4. The following information is known about them. Rashmi won the race and was wearing a Red coloured Jersey. The girl, who came third in the race, wore the jersey numbered ‘1’. Rukmini finished the race ahead of the girl wearing the Yellow jersey but she wasn’t wearing the jersey numbered ‘2’. Only one girl finished in the same position as the number written on her jersey. Rajni beat the girl wearing the jersey numbered ‘3’ and Rosy wore a Yellow jersey. One of the four girls wore a Blue coloured jersey. The Green coloured jersey had ‘2’ written over it.","setup_solution":"After observing the data, we can observe the following points. (a) Rashmi wore the Red coloured jersey and came 1st. (b) Rukmini and Rajni finished either 2nd or 3rd as each of them is ahead of one other person (hence they did not finish 4th). (c) Rosy wore a Yellow jersey and she finished 4th. (d) Jersey numbered 1 finished 3rd. We know that only one girl’s jersey number is the same as the position in which she finished and that is not 1. (Neither can it be 3 as per (d) above). Rajni can either be 2nd or 3rd and she is ahead of the person wearing the jersey numbered 3, which cannot be the person who finished 3rd. So it must be the person who finished 4th i.e. Rosy, who wears the jersey numbered 3. Therefore, Rosy is wearing Yellow, finishes 4th and wears the jersey numbered 3. The person whose jersey number and the position is the same must be the jersey numbered 2 who finished 2nd. This person is not Rukmini (as it is given that Rukmini wasn’t wearing the jersey numbered 2) which means it must be Rajni. The final arrangement: Jersey Number 4, Colour Red, Position 1, Person Rashmi; Jersey Number 2, Colour Green, Position 2, Person Rajni; Jersey Number 1, Colour Blue, Position 3, Person Rukmini; Jersey Number 3, Colour Yellow, Position 4, Person Rosy.","entity_count":4,"variables":3,"constraints":7,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3A_Q013","question_text":"Who wore the Blue jersey?","options":{"A":"Rashmi","B":"Rajni","C":"Rukmini","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Rukmini","solution_text":"Rukmini wore the Blue jersey.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q014","question_text":"Which two girls were wearing jerseys numbered ‘1’ and ‘2’?","options":{"A":"Rashmi and Rajni","B":"Rukmini and Rashmi","C":"Rajni and Rukmini","D":"Rosy and Rajini"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Rajni and Rukmini","solution_text":"Jersies numbered 1 and 2 were worn by Rukmini and Rajni.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q015","question_text":"Which of the following is true?","options":{"A":"Rashmi wore the jersey numbered ‘4’ and finished in 2nd position.","B":"Rosy wore the jersey numbered ‘3’ and she finished 3rd.","C":"Rajni wore the Green jersey and did not finish 3rd.","D":"Jersey number of Rajni is 1 and she finished 2nd."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Rajni wore the Green jersey and did not finish 3rd.","solution_text":"'Rajni wore a Green jersey and did not finish 3rd' is true.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3A_S16_18","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Each of the twelve persons were born on one of the two dates 2nd and 19th of one of six months among – January, February, May, August, September, and November - in the same year, not necessarily in the same order. No two persons were born on the same day of the same month. One of the persons is Vasu. The following information is known about them. Note 1: The persons whose name starts with a letter which comes after ‘L’ as per the English alphabetical series was born in the first half of the year i.e., from January to June. The persons whose name starts with a letter which comes before ‘L’ as per the English alphabetical series was born in the second half of the year i.e., from July to December. Note 2: The person whose name starts with a vowel was born in the month which has an odd number of days. The person whose name starts with a consonant was born in the month which has an even number of days. Only one person was born between Giridar and Ishan, who was born immediately after Omkar. Harish was born after Karthik. The number of persons who were born after Harish is the same as the number of persons who were born before Ompati. Mahesh was born after Umesh and before Upendra but not immediately before. Achyut and Devesh were born on different dates.","setup_solution":"From the given information, only one person was born between Giridar and Ishan, who was born immediately after Omkar. From this, we can say that Omkar and Ishan were born on 19th May and 2nd August respectively. Giridar was born on 2nd September. It is given that Harish was born after Karthik. From this, we can say that Karthik was born on either 19th September or 2nd November and Harish was born on either 2nd or 19th November. From this, we get the following possibilities. Case1: Omkar-19May, Ishan-2Aug, Giridar-2Sep, Karthik-19Sep, Harish-2Nov. Case2: Omkar-19May, Ishan-2Aug, Giridar-2Sep, Karthik-19Sep, Harish-19Nov. Case3: Omkar-19May, Ishan-2Aug, Giridar-2Sep, Karthik-2Nov, Harish-19Nov. It is given that the number of persons born after Harish is the same as the number of persons born before Ompati. From this, we can say that Ompati was born on either 2nd or 19th January. From the given information, Mahesh was born after Umesh and before Upendra but not immediately before Upendra. From this, we can say that in case 1, Umesh was born on 2nd January. Mahesh and Upendra were born on 2nd February and 2nd May respectively. In case 2 and 3, Umesh was born on 19th January. Mahesh and Upendra were born on 2nd February and 2nd May respectively. Hence in any case February month has 28 days. Given that Achyut and Devesh were born on different dates. From this, we can say that case 1 and 3 are eliminated as we cannot place Devesh. In case 2, Achyut and Devesh were born on 19th August and 2nd November respectively. Vasu was born on 19th February. The final arrangement: Ompati-2Jan, Umesh-19Jan, Mahesh-2Feb, Vasu-19Feb, Upendra-2May, Omkar-19May, Ishan-2Aug, Achyut-19Aug, Giridar-2Sep, Karthik-19Sep, Devesh-2Nov, Harish-19Nov.","entity_count":12,"variables":2,"constraints":8,"case_splits":3,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["sequential_ordering","case_analysis"],"type_specific":{"note":"Total person count is unstated — must be derived from the constraints. Alphabetical-name rules (Note 1 and Note 2) add a meta-constraint layer: first-letter of name determines which half of year and which month-length category a person belongs to, requiring name analysis before any placement can begin."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3A_Q016","question_text":"Who was born on 19th August?","options":{"A":"Ishan","B":"Umesh","C":"Omkar","D":"Achyut"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Achyut","solution_text":"Achyut was born on 19th August.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q017","question_text":"How many persons were born before Karthik?","options":{"A":"Nine","B":"Eight","C":"Six","D":"Seven"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Nine","solution_text":"Nine persons were born before Karthik.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q018","question_text":"How many days are there between the dates on which Upendra and Achyut were born?","options":{"A":"100","B":"49","C":"108","D":"105"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"108","solution_text":"108 days are there between the dates on which Upendra and Achyut were born. Months May June July August: days 29 30 31 18. 29 + 30 + 31 + 18 = 108 days.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3A_S19_21","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Each of the five executives A, B, C, D and E, of a company went to a different place among Mumbai, Kolkata, Delhi, Chennai and Bengaluru. Each of them stayed in a different hotel among Taj, Grand, Oberoi, Basera and Jahanuma. Each of these hotels is either a four star hotel or a five star hotel. The following information is known. (i) A went to Delhi but did not stay in Oberoi hotel. (ii) The person who went to Mumbai stayed in Taj Hotel. (iii) Among these five hotels the number of 5 star hotels is more than the number of 4 star hotels. (iv) Basera and Jahanuma are four star hotels. (v) C stayed in a 5 star hotel which is neither in Mumbai nor in Kolkata. (vi) D went to Bengaluru and stayed in a four star hotel which is not Basera. (vii) E did not stay in a 5 star hotel.","setup_solution":"From (ii) and (iv), as the number of 5 star hotels is more than the number of 4 star hotels and Basera and Jahanuma are four star hotels, Grand, Oberoi and Taj are five star hotels. From (vi) as D went to Bengaluru and stayed in a four star hotel which is not Basera, the hotel is Jahanuma. From (vii) E stayed in a four star hotel, E stayed at Basera. From (ii) Taj is in Mumbai and C did not stay there (from (v)). From (i) A went to Delhi and the hotel is not Oberoi. Hence, it is Grand. B went to Mumbai and stayed at Taj. C went to Chennai and stayed at Oberoi. E went to Kolkata. The complete distribution table: A-Delhi-Grand-5star; B-Mumbai-Taj-5star; C-Chennai-Oberoi-5star; D-Bengaluru-Jahanuma-4star; E-Kolkata-Basera-4star.","entity_count":5,"variables":3,"constraints":7,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3A_Q019","question_text":"Who went to Chennai?","options":{"A":"B","B":"E","C":"C","D":"D"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"C","solution_text":"C went to Chennai.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q020","question_text":"Where is the Grand Hotel?","options":{"A":"Kolkata","B":"Delhi","C":"Chennai","D":"Bangaluru"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Delhi","solution_text":"Grand Hotel is in Delhi.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q021","question_text":"Which of the following is the correct combination of hotel, place and person respectively?","options":{"A":"B – Mumbai – Taj","B":"Basera – Kolkata – D","C":"Oberoi – Chennai – B","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"B – Mumbai – Taj","solution_text":"None of the given options represents the correct combination of hotel, place and name in that order.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3A_S22_24","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Each of the six friends-Tanvi, Toshi, Rupa, Renu, Jaya and Bina are students of an Engineering college. Each of them belong to a different state among M.P., U.P., A.P., T.N., H.P., and W.B. and each of them is studying a different branch of engineering among Electronics, Computer Science, Information Technology, Electrical, Mechanical and Civil. Following is the information known about them. (i) Rupa is from U.P and the person from M.P is studying Information Technology. (ii) Renu is studying Electronics but is not from W.B. (iii) Tanvi is from H.P but is not studying Civil Engineering. (iv) The person from T.N. is not studying Electronics. (v) Toshi is neither from W.B. nor studying Information Technology. (vi) Bina is studying Computer Science. (vii) The person from T.N. is studying neither Mechanical engineering nor in Civil Engineering.","setup_solution":"The given information is written in the following table. From the above table, the person from M.P. is not Toshi and also cannot be Rupa, Renu, Tanvi or Bina. Jaya is from M.P. Hence, she is studying Information Technology. The person from W.B. cannot be any one among Rupa, Renu, Jaya, Tanvi and Toshi. Bina is from W.B. Toshi is from T.N. Hence she is studying neither Mechanical nor Civil Engineering. Renu is from A.P. Neither Tanvi nor Toshi is studying Civil Engineering. Hence, Rupa is studying Civil Engineering. As Toshi is not studying Mechanical Engineering, Tanvi is studying Mechanical Engineering. The distribution table: Rupa-U.P.-Civil; Jaya-M.P.-Information Tech; Renu-A.P.-Electronics; Tanvi-H.P.-Mechanical; Bina-W.B.-Computer Science; Toshi-T.N.-Electrical.","entity_count":6,"variables":2,"constraints":7,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3A_Q022","question_text":"Which branch is Toshi studying?","options":{"A":"Mechanical","B":"Civil","C":"Electrical","D":"Data inadequate"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Electrical","solution_text":"Toshi is studying Electrical Engineering.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q023","question_text":"The person who is studying the Mechanical branch is from ________","options":{"A":"T.N.","B":"U.P","C":"A.P.","D":"H.P."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"H.P.","solution_text":"The person from H.P is studying Mechanical Branch.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q024","question_text":"Which of the following is the correct combination of the person, state and branch respectively?","options":{"A":"Rupa – U.P. - Mechanical","B":"Bina – T.N. – Computer Science.","C":"Jaya – M.P. – Information Technology.","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Jaya – M.P. – Information Technology.","solution_text":"Jaya – M.P. – Information Technology is the correct combination.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3A_S25_27","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Two male singers – Kailash and Sonu and two female singers Shreya and Kavita are singing for four different movies – M1, M2, M3 and M4 of four different directors – D1, D2, D3 and D4. In each movie a male singer and a female singer worked, but no two movies has the same pair of male and female singers. We know the following information. (i) Neither M1 nor M3 is directed by D3 or D4. (ii) The male singer for director D4 is Kailash and the female singer for M3 is not Shreya. (iii) Kavita and Sonu work together in M2. (iv) M3 is the movie directed by D2.","setup_solution":"From (i), either D1 or D2 are the director for movies M1 and M3 and either D3 or D4 are the directors of the movies M2 and M4. From (iv) M3 is directed by D2 which implies that M1 is directed by D1. From (ii) and (iii) Kavita and Sonu worked together in M2, and for D4 Kailash is the male singer, which means that M2 is not directed by D4. Hence, M2 is directed by D3 and M4 by D4. Now the singers for M3 are Kavita and Kailash and for M1 are Shreya and Sonu. Movie-Director-Male-Female: M1-D1-Sonu-Shreya; M2-D3-Sonu-Kavita; M3-D2-Kailash-Kavita; M4-D4-Kailash-Shreya.","entity_count":4,"variables":3,"constraints":4,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3A_Q025","question_text":"Who is the director of the movie M4?","options":{"A":"D2","B":"D3","C":"D4","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"D4","solution_text":"D4 is the director of M4.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q026","question_text":"Which pair of singers work together in movie M3?","options":{"A":"Kailash – Shreya","B":"Kailash – Kavitha","C":"Sonu – Shreya","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Kailash – Kavitha","solution_text":"Kailash and Kavitha work together in M3.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q027","question_text":"In which movie does the pair Sonu and Shreya worked together?","options":{"A":"M2","B":"M3","C":"M1","D":"M4"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"M1","solution_text":"Sonu and Shreya work together in M1.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3A_S28_30","exercise":"exercise_3a","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Seven persons – F, G, H, I, J, K and L – own three different types of cars among - Swift, Ford and Polo. Each person drove for a different distance among – 16, 23, 26, 31, 38, 42 and 51 km – but not necessarily in the same order. At least two persons own each type of car. The following information is known about them. G and the person who drove for 31 km own either Polo or Ford and do not own the same car. Neither L nor the person who drove 26 km owns Polo. J and K own the same car and both drove a distance which is an odd number. The sum of the distances driven by I and J is 65 km. The person who drove a distance which is a perfect square owns Polo. H drove greater distance than I. F and I own the same car. The person who drove for 38 km does not own Swift.","setup_solution":"From the information, G and the person who drove for 31 km owns either Polo or Ford and does not own the same car. Neither L nor the person who drove for 26 km owns Polo. We can say that, G owns either Polo or Ford, and the person who drove for 31 km owns either Ford or Polo. G did not drive for 31 km. L and the person who drove for 26 km own either Ford or Swift. L did not drive for 26 km. From the information, J and K own the same type of car and both driven for distance which is an odd number, J and K driven 23 or 31 or 51 km. From the above information we get the following arrangement. From the information, the sum of the distances driven by I and J is 65 km, we can say that, I and J driven for 42 and 23 km respectively. K driven for either 31 or 51 km. It is also given that, the person who driven for a distance perfect square owns Polo. From this, the person who driven for 16 km owns Polo. F or G or H driven for 16 km. From the information, H driven more distance than I, we can say that, H driven for 51 km. Thus K and L driven for 31 and 38 km respectively. J and K own either Ford or Polo. It is also given that, F and I own the same car. The person who driven for 38 km does not own Swift, we can say that, L owns Ford. F does not driven for 16 km, as more than three persons will own the same type of car. Thus G driven for 16 km and owns Polo. H owns Polo. J and K own Ford. The final arrangement: F-Swift-26; G-Polo-16; H-Polo-51; I-Swift-42; J-Ford-23; K-Ford-31; L-Ford-38.","entity_count":7,"variables":2,"constraints":8,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination","case_analysis"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3A_Q028","question_text":"Who among the following owns Swift?","options":{"A":"H","B":"I","C":"F","D":"Both (B) and (C)"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Both (B) and (C)","solution_text":"I and F own Swift.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q029","question_text":"What is the difference between the distances driven by G and I?","options":{"A":"26","B":"19","C":"7","D":"10"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"26","solution_text":"The difference between the distances driven by G and I is 42–16 = 26.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3A_Q030","question_text":"Four of the following five are alike in a certain way and hence form a group. Find the one which does not belong to that group.","options":{"A":"F","B":"G","C":"H","D":"I"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"H","solution_text":"Except H, all the other persons have driven for distances which are even numbers.","question_operation":"odd_one_out","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3B_S1_3","exercise":"exercise_3b","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. There are six offices – P, Q, R, S, T and U – in each of which one employee out of A, B, C, D, E and F works. No employee works in two offices and no office has any two of these employees. If A works in R, then D works in T. Either C or E works in S. Only if F works in R, then B works in P. D works in Q, and either B or F works in U. Either A or F works in P.","setup_solution":"Given that: (ii) If A = R, then D = T. (iii) Either C = S, or E = S. (iv) Only if F = R, then B = P. (v) D = Q. (vi) Either B = U, or F = U. (vii) Either A = P, or F = P. Now, it is given that D works in Q (v), then D does not work in T, which means that A does not work in R (q → ¬p). Also, from (iii) we can conclude that neither of A, B, D and F works in S. Similarly, from (vi), neither of A, C, D and E works in U. From (vii), neither of B, C, D and E works in P.","entity_count":6,"variables":2,"constraints":6,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["conditional_logic","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3B_Q001","question_text":"If C works in R, then which of the following statements is definitely true?","options":{"A":"B works in U.","B":"A works in P.","C":"E works in T.","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"None of these","solution_text":"If C works in R, then E works in S (iii). As can be observed, B could be working in T or U; A could be working in P or T and F could be working in P, T or U. Hence, none of the given statements are definitely true.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q002","question_text":"Which of the following helps in completing the arrangement?","options":{"A":"F works in T and A works in P.","B":"C works in T and B works in P.","C":"B works in U and E works in R.","D":"F works in P and C works in S."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"F works in P and C works in S.","solution_text":"(A) If F works in T and A works in P, then B works in U (vi). But C works in R or S. Also E works in R or S. Hence, the arrangement is not completed. (B) 'B works in P' is not possible, because as per (vii), either A or F works in P. (C) 'B works in U' means F does not work in U (each office has only one out of these six workers) and 'E works in R' means C works in S(iii). But still, for A and F, it is undecided as to who out of these two works in P and who works in T. (D) 'F works in P' means A works in T and 'C works in S' means that E works in R, then B works in U. Hence, the arrangement is completed by using this statement.","question_operation":"identify_completing_statement","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q003","question_text":"If F does not work in P and C works in R, then what is the total number of possible arrangements?","options":{"A":"4","B":"3","C":"2","D":"1"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"2","solution_text":"As F does not work with P, hence A must be working with P (vii) and as C works with R, hence E must be working with S(iii). Now, either B works with U and F works with T, or B works with T and F works with U. Hence, there are two arrangements which are possible.","question_operation":"count_arrangements","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3B_S4_6","exercise":"exercise_3b","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Ten monkeys A through J visit a garden which has tree bearing fruits viz, Mango, Guava, Banana and Berry. Further it is known that (i) Only one monkey visited all the trees. (ii) Every tree is visited by six monkeys. (iii) A, D, E, G, I, J visited the Guava tree. (iv) D, F, G did not visit the Berry tree. (v) B, C, D, G, I, J visited the Banana tree. (vi) J, A, H did not visit the Mango tree. (vii) Every monkey visited at least one tree and exactly two monkeys visited one tree only.","setup_solution":"Given, Every tree is visited by 6 monkeys, total sum of the number of trees visited by all the monkeys is 24 i.e., 4 × 6. The monkey that visited all the trees cannot be D as it did not visit the Berry tree, it cannot be G as it did not visit the Berry tree, it cannot be J as it did not visit the Mango tree. The monkey that visited all the trees is I. It is known that the number of monkeys that visited all the trees is 1 and those that visited only one tree is 2. Let the number of monkeys that visited 3 trees be x and those that visited 2 trees be y. 1(4) + x(3) + y(2) + 2(1) = 24 → 3x + 2y = 18 and we know that x + y = 7 → x = 4. From the given data, the monkeys that visited only one tree must be F and H as F did not visit Guava, Berry and Banana trees and H did not visit Guava, Banana and Mango trees. A must visit at least two trees as it did not visit the Banana and Mango tree, means it should visit the Berry and Guava trees.","entity_count":10,"variables":2,"constraints":7,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["set_membership"],"type_specific":{"note":"Set membership with counting constraint — total visits = 24 (4 trees × 6 monkeys each), distributed across 10 monkeys under the 'at least one, exactly two visit one only' rule. Requires solving a simultaneous equation (3x + 2y = 18, x + y = 7) to determine how many monkeys visited 3 trees vs 2 trees."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3B_Q004","question_text":"Which of the following monkeys visited all the trees?","options":{"A":"D","B":"G","C":"I","D":"J"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"I","solution_text":"The monkey that visited all the trees is I.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q005","question_text":"How many monkeys visited exactly three trees?","options":{"A":"3","B":"4","C":"5","D":"2"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"4","solution_text":"x = 4.","question_operation":"count_arrangements","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q006","question_text":"Which of the following monkeys have visited the Berry tree?","options":{"A":"B","B":"C","C":"A","D":"F"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"A","solution_text":"A must visit at least two trees as it did not visit the Banana and Mango tree, means it should visit the Berry and Guava trees.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3B_S7_9","exercise":"exercise_3b","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Nine persons, K through S, visited three different cities among Jammu, Nashik, and Kollam. Each person is of a different age. At least two and at most, four persons visited a city. Note: If it is given that K and the person who is 30 years old visited the same city, which means K and the person who is 30 years old are different persons. Q visited Nashik with the person whose age is 32 years. M is younger than the persons who visited Nashik. M did not visit Nashik. L and the person whose age is 42 years visited the same city. L visited a city that neither Q nor M visited. The person who is the youngest among all the nine persons visited Jammu. M is 4 years younger than P. M and P visited the same city. P’s age is equal to the average age of the person who visited Nashik. K and N visited the same city. O’s age is 35 years, and he is the youngest among the persons who visited the same city as O. The persons whose ages are 27 and 28 years visited the same city. R’s age is either 27 or 28, but he is 4 years younger than S. R and P visited different cities. K is the oldest and is 12 years older than L. K’s age is twice the age of the person who is the youngest among all the 9 persons.","setup_solution":"It is given that Q visited Nashik with the person whose age is 32 years. From this, we can say that Q and the person who is 32 years old visited Nashik. M is younger than the person who visited Nashik. M did not visit Nashik. L and the person whose age is 42 years visited the same city. L visited a city that neither Q nor M visited. From this, we can say that in case 1, M visited Jammu and in case 2, M visited Kollam. In case 1, L and the person whose age is 42 years visited Kollam. In case 2, L and the person whose age is 42 years visited Jammu. M is 4 years younger than P. M and P visited the same city. P’s age is equal to the average age of the person who visited Nashik. O’s age is 35 years, and he is the youngest among the persons who visited the same city as O. Therefore, the only possibility is O visited Kollam. From this, we can say that case 2 is eliminated. In case 1, O visited Kollam. K and N visited the same city. The persons whose ages are 27 and 28 visited the same city. R’s age is either 27 or 28, but he is 4 years younger than S. R and P visited different cities. R’s age is 28 and S’s age is 32. Since K and N visited the same city, the only possibility is that they visited Kollam. P’s age is (27+32+28)/3 = 87/3 = 29. P’s age is 29 years and M’s age is 25 years. K is the oldest and 12 years older than L. K’s age is twice the age of the youngest person. K’s age is 50 years (M is the youngest). L’s and N’s ages are 38 and 42 respectively. Final arrangement: Jammu: M-25, P-29; Nashik: Q-27, S-32, R-28; Kollam: L-38, O-35, K-50, N-42.","entity_count":9,"variables":2,"constraints":12,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination","case_analysis"],"type_specific":{"note":"Numeric age attributes must be derived from algebraic relationships (averages, differences, multiples) before they can be used as positional constraints. This adds a calculation layer on top of the standard constraint-elimination grid."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3B_Q007","question_text":"Who among the following persons visited Nashik?","options":{"A":"L","B":"O","C":"P","D":"S"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"S","solution_text":"S visited Nashik.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q008","question_text":"What is the sum of the ages (in years) of the persons who visited Kollam?","options":{"A":"163","B":"166","C":"162","D":"165"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"165","solution_text":"The sum of the ages (in years) of the persons who visited Kollam is 38 + 35 + 50 + 42 = 165.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q009","question_text":"What is the difference between the ages of the oldest and the youngest persons?","options":{"A":"25 years","B":"22 years","C":"20 years","D":"26 years"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"25 years","solution_text":"The difference between the ages of the oldest and the youngest persons is 50 – 25 = 25.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3B_S10_12","exercise":"exercise_3b","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight students − A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H − went to four different places among Resort, Beach, Hotel and Cinema, such that each place was visited by two students each. Each student visited exactly one place. After their return, their teacher asked them about the place visited by each of them. Following were their answers: (i) A said “I did not go with C or D and went to the Resort or the Cinema”. (ii) B said “I did not go with E or G and went to the Hotel or the Cinema. (iii) C said “I did not go with D or F and went to the Beach or the Resort. (iv) D said “I did not go with B or H and went to the Beach or the Hotel”. (v) E said “I went with B or C or D or F or H and went to the Cinema or the Beach”. (vi) F said “I did not go with A or G and went to the Resort or the Cinema”. (vii) G said “I went with B or D or E or F or H and went to the Beach or the Hotel”. (viii) H said “I did not go with C or A and went to the Resort or the Beach”.","setup_solution":"From (i) and (v), we get A and E do not go with each other. From (i) and (vi), we get A and F do not go with each other. From (i) and (vii), we get A and G do not go with each other. From (i) and (vii), we get A and H do not go with each other. Hence, A and B go together. Similarly, we can determine the names of the other persons who go together. A and B go to the Cinema. C and E go to the Beach. D and G go to the Hotel. F and H go to the Resort.","entity_count":8,"variables":2,"constraints":8,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination"],"type_specific":{"note":"All constraints are given as student self-reports (I did not go with X, I went to Y or Z), requiring symmetric inference — if A says 'not with C', and C says 'not with F', both constraints must be cross-checked simultaneously to identify valid pairings rather than processing each student's statement independently."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3B_Q010","question_text":"Who among the following went with A?","options":{"A":"E","B":"B","C":"G","D":"H"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"B","solution_text":"B goes with A.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q011","question_text":"E went with _____ and visited the _____.","options":{"A":"C, Beach","B":"F, Cinema","C":"D, Beach","D":"G, Beach"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"C, Beach","solution_text":"E went with C and they visited the Beach.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q012","question_text":"If only D and H lied about the places visited by them, then with whom did D visit the place of his choice?","options":{"A":"H","B":"F","C":"G","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"F","solution_text":"If D and H lied about the places they visited, then H goes along with G to the Hotel and D goes along with F to the Resort.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3B_S13_17","exercise":"exercise_3b","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. A group of six persons – Babar, Humayun, Akbar, Jahangir, Shahjahan and Aurangjeb, visited Angel world, where there were six rides – R1 through R6, among which two were for children, two for adults and two were common for all. There were three children and three adults in the group. Each of them went for at least three rides. The cost of each ride is ₹10 per person. Before going for any adults’ or any children’s ride one must go for at least one of the common rides. Before going for R6 one must go for R3. Four persons went for both R1 and R2. Akbar did not go for R1 or R6. Shahjahan went for both R6 and R3. R4 is a children’s ride. No adult went for children’s ride and no child went for an adult’s ride. They spent a total amount of ₹200 on these rides. No two of them have same combination.","setup_solution":"Cost of each ride per person = ₹10. Total cost = ₹200. Total number of rides = 20. Each person must have visited at least three rides and at most four rides. To get 20 rides from 6 persons, there must be four persons who went for 3 rides only and rest 2 persons went for four rides each and those two persons, who went for four rides each, both cannot be children or adults. As both R1 and R2 were taken by 4 persons, neither can be either children’s or adult’s ride. R1 and R2 are common rides. As Shahjahan went for both R3 and R6 and R4 is a children’s ride, Shahjahan is an adult. Since, Akbar did not go for R1 or R6, he is a child and went for R2, R4 and R5.","entity_count":6,"variables":3,"constraints":9,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["conditional_logic","constraint_elimination"],"type_specific":{"note":"Prerequisite-order constraints (must do common ride before adult/child ride; must do R3 before R6) function like sequential dependencies layered on top of a standard distribution grid, requiring the solver to track ordering constraints not just assignment constraints."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3B_Q013","question_text":"What percentage of total expenditure did they spend on common rides?","options":{"A":"40","B":"50","C":"55","D":"25"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"50","solution_text":"As each person went for at least three rides, each person must take one common ride. As four persons went for R1 and R2 (common rides), the other two should have one common ride each. A total of ten common rides. Total expenditure on common rides = 10 × 10 = ₹100. Required percentage = (100/200) × 100 = 50%.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q014","question_text":"If Humayun was an adult, which of the following rides he must have went for?","options":{"A":"R3","B":"R6","C":"R5","D":"R2"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"R3","solution_text":"R3 must be taken before R6. If Humayun is an adult he must have taken R3.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q015","question_text":"If Aurangjeb and Babar were children, then what was the least number of rides which were rode by both of them?","options":{"A":"0","B":"1","C":"2","D":"3"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"2","solution_text":"As each of them went for at least three rides and there are only four rides for children, in each pair of children, each child must have at least two rides which the other child went for. There must be at least two common rides.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q016","question_text":"If Jahangir did not go for R2, then Humayun must have went for ______","options":{"A":"R2","B":"R3","C":"R4","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"R2","solution_text":"From the above solution, we can say that four persons went for both R1 and R2 and the remaining two persons have one common ride each. But these two persons don’t have same common ride. Since, Jahangir did not go for R2, he must have went for R1. Now Akbar and Jahangir are the two persons who went for one common round each. Hence, the remaining four went for both the common rides. Thus, Humayun went for R2.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q017","question_text":"Which of the following statements will give us a clear distribution?","options":{"A":"Both Shahjahan and Aurangjeb went for four rides each.","B":"Babar, Akbar and Shahjahan were children.","C":"No ride was visited by both Akbar and Jahangeer.","D":"None of the above"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"None of the above","solution_text":"Choice (A) does not give us the clear idea about the rides covered by Babar and Humayun. Choice (B) does not give us the clear idea about the pattern of the rides they covered. Choice (C) There is no idea about children and adults.","question_operation":"identify_completing_statement","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3B_S18_21","exercise":"exercise_3b","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight persons – P to W – study in three different colleges among A, B, and C and three different streams among X, Y, and Z, but not necessarily in the same order. No two persons who study the same stream study in the same college. At least two persons and at most three students study each stream and in each college. P does not study in college C and Q does not study stream Y. R and Q study in the same college; P and V study the same stream. T and W study in the same college. S studies either stream Y or stream Z. U and Q study the same stream. R studies stream Z and in college A. V does not study stream Y. T studies in either the college A or college C. S and W study the same stream.","setup_solution":"It is given that R studies stream Z and in college A. R and Q study in same college. Hence, Q studies in A but Q does not study stream Z and Q studies stream X and Q and U study the same stream hence U also studies stream X. P and V study the same stream but V does not study stream Y. Hence P and V study stream Z. S studies either stream Y or Z but S cannot study Z hence S studies stream Y. S and W study same stream, Hence, W also studies Y. T and W study in the same college. T studies in either A or C but T cannot study in college A. T and W study in C. T cannot study stream Y or Z, hence T studies X. S can study in either A or B. U studies in college C. The final distribution: P-Z-B; Q-X-A; R-Z-A; S-Y-A/B; T-X-C; U-X-B; V-Z-C; W-Y-C.","entity_count":8,"variables":2,"constraints":10,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":2,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3B_Q018","question_text":"Who among the following studies stream Y?","options":{"A":"P","B":"S","C":"U","D":"Q"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"S","solution_text":"S studies stream Y.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q019","question_text":"In which of the following streams does T study?","options":{"A":"Z","B":"Y","C":"X","D":"Either X or Z"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"X","solution_text":"T studies stream X.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q020","question_text":"Who among the following studies in college B?","options":{"A":"P","B":"R","C":"T","D":"V"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"P","solution_text":"P studies in college B.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q021","question_text":"Which of the following ‘person – stream – college’ combinations is definitely true?","options":{"A":"S – Y – B","B":"T – X – B","C":"V – Y – C","D":"U – X – B"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"U – X – B","solution_text":"U – X – B combination is true.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3B_S22_24","exercise":"exercise_3b","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight employees – P through W of a company went to camps to different places among – Bangalore, Hyderabad, Mumbai and Delhi in different months among – April, September, October and December in a year. Those employees who went to the same place, did not go in the same month. Exactly two employees went in the same month and exactly two employees went to the same place. T went to Delhi, in either October or April. V went in September to neither Hyderabad nor Mumbai. One of the employees who went to Bangalore went in December. S went in December. U and R went to the same place, R and W went in the same month. P, R and Q went to different places, but not to Mumbai and they went in different months, but not in April. P did not go in September.","setup_solution":"The given information can be tabulated. Given, U and R went to the same place. Hence, U should not go to Mumbai and S and W went to Mumbai, U and R went to Hyderabad, since only one of P, Q and R went to Hyderabad and exactly two employees went to the same place. Given, R and W went in the same month. Hence it is not April and also neither December nor September, since, exactly two employees went in the same month. Hence, it is October. Hence T went in April, P went to Bangalore in December, Q went in September, U went in April and V went to Bangalore. The final distribution: P-Bangalore-December; Q-Delhi-September; R-Hyderabad-October; S-Mumbai-December; T-Delhi-April; U-Hyderabad-April; V-Bangalore-September; W-Mumbai-October.","entity_count":8,"variables":2,"constraints":9,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3B_Q022","question_text":"Who among the following went to Delhi?","options":{"A":"P","B":"Q","C":"R","D":"V"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Q","solution_text":"Q went to Delhi.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q023","question_text":"Who among the following went in October?","options":{"A":"P","B":"Q","C":"R","D":"T"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"R","solution_text":"R went in October.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q024","question_text":"Which among the following group of employees went to the same place?","options":{"A":"P, S","B":"Q, T","C":"V, Q","D":"S, T"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Q, T","solution_text":"(Q, T) went to the same place.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3B_S25_27","exercise":"exercise_3b","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Twelve persons – L through W – were born in two consecutive years, 2024 and 2025. Each person was born on the same date but in different months. No two persons were born in the same month of any year. The following information is known about them. P was born in a month that has an even number of days and in the year that has an odd number of days. There are six months between the months in which P and U were born. U and P were born in different years. U was born in a month that has 30 days. T was born in 2024 in a month that has 30 days which comes after June. O is four months older than T. N is older than V. The months in which N and V were born occur consecutively in a calendar and N and V were born in different years. The number of days in the month in which N and V were born are distinct odd numbers. M was in the month which is between the months in which V and P were born. W is older than M. The number of months between the months in which N and W were born is thrice the number of persons younger than W. At least two persons were born between the months in which U and W were born. S is five months older than Q. L is older than R and was born in a month that has 31 days.","setup_solution":"It is given that P was born in a month that has an even number of days and in the year that has an odd number of days. There are six months between the months in which P and U were born. U and P were born in different years. U was born in a month that has 30 days. From this, we get two possibilities: in case 1, P was born in April 2025 and U was born in September 2024. In case 2, P was born in June 2025 and U was born in November 2024. T was born in 2024 in a month that has 30 days which comes after June. In case 1, T was born in November. In case 2, T was born in September. O is four months older than T. In case 1, O was born in July 2024. In case 2, O was born in May 2024. N is older than V. The months in which N and V were born occur consecutively in a calendar and N and V were born in different years. The number of days in the months in which N and V were born are odd but not the same. In both cases, N was born in February 2024. V was born in either January or March of 2025. M was born in the month which is between the months in which V and P were born. In case 1, V and M were born in January 2025 and March 2025 respectively. W is older than M. The number of months between the months in which N and W were born is thrice the number of persons younger than W. In case 1, W was born in December 2024 and only three persons were born after W. In case 2, V was born in January 2025. W and M were born in March 2025 and April 2025 respectively and only four persons were born after W. S is five months older than Q. In case 1, S and Q were born in May and October 2024 respectively. In case 2, S and Q were born in July 2024 and December 2024 respectively. L is older than R and was born in a month that has 31 days. Case 1 is eliminated as we cannot place R. Since only three persons are younger than W. In case 2, L and R were born in August 2025 and October 2025 respectively. Final arrangement: 2024: Feb-N, May-O, Jul-S, Sep-T, Nov-U, Dec-Q. 2025: Jan-V, Mar-W, Apr-M, Jun-P, Aug-L, Oct-R.","entity_count":12,"variables":2,"constraints":12,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["sequential_ordering","case_analysis"],"type_specific":{"note":"Two-year calendar grid with 12 persons placed across 12 distinct month-year slots (one per month per year, no repeats). Month-property rules (odd/even days, month length) constrain which calendar positions are available to each person before any direct placement clue applies — requires knowing calendar properties before solving."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3B_Q025","question_text":"In which month was L born?","options":{"A":"October","B":"May","C":"August","D":"March"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"August","solution_text":"L was born in August.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q026","question_text":"How many persons are younger than U?","options":{"A":"Three","B":"Four","C":"Six","D":"Seven"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Seven","solution_text":"Seven persons are younger than U.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q027","question_text":"Who among the following persons was older than S?","options":{"A":"N","B":"O","C":"U","D":"Both (A) and (B)"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Both (A) and (B)","solution_text":"Both N and O were older than S.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E3B_S28_30","exercise":"exercise_3b","type":"distribution","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Five friends Roy, Boy, Yoy, Goy and Moy went to purchase backpacks, for themselves of the same brand and design but different colors among: Red, Blue, Yellow, Green and Maroon. Further, the following information is known: (A) No person’s first letter of the name matched with that of color of the bags they bought. (B) Yoy hates Green and Maroon and so did not purchase it. (C) Roy and Boy like the green color of the backpack their friend bought. (D) Goy and her friend purchase the backpack in such a manner that the color of Goy’s backpack matches with the first letter of her friend’s name, while her friend purchased a green backpack.","setup_solution":"The given conditions and information can be tabulated as below: Person Backpack Color: Yoy R/B; Roy Y/B; Boy R/Y; Goy M; Moy G.","entity_count":5,"variables":2,"constraints":4,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["conditional_logic","constraint_elimination"],"type_specific":{"note":"First-letter-of-name matches first-letter-of-color constraint (condition A) and Goy's friend condition (condition D) require reasoning about letter-name correspondences rather than purely categorical assignments. Hybrid logical-linguistic puzzle."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E3B_Q028","question_text":"If Roy exchanged his backpack with Yoy, and then Yoy exchanged his backpack with Boy, and after these exchanges were made, they all had backpacks with the first letters of their names matching with the first letters of the colors of the backpacks, then which of the following is true?","options":{"A":"Boy purchased a red backpack.","B":"Boy purchased a yellow backpack.","C":"Yoy purchased a blue backpack.","D":"Roy purchased a yellow backpack."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Boy purchased a yellow backpack.","solution_text":"(1) Roy exchanged his backpack with Yoy and (2) Yoy exchanged his backpack with Boy. Since Yoy gets Yellow after the second exchange, Boy’s backpack is yellow, and since Roy’s backpack is red after exchange, Yoy’s backpack should be red and therefore, Yoy must have bought blue backpack.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q029","question_text":"Which of the following statements is not necessarily true?","options":{"A":"Either Roy or Moy bought a green backpack.","B":"Yoy bought a red or a blue backpack.","C":"Roy or Boy bought a yellow backpack.","D":"Roy or Goy bought a blue backpack."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Roy or Goy bought a blue backpack.","solution_text":"Statement (A) is true. Statement (B) is true. Statement (C) is true. Statement (D) is not necessarily true.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E3B_Q030","question_text":"If the colour of back packs of two friends are such that, after interchanging their backpacks color of exactly one person’s backpack and her name started with the same letter. However, conditions (b) to (d) are not violated. Which of the following lets you completely determine the colors of backpacks bought by all five friends?","options":{"A":"The interchange should took place between Goy and Moy.","B":"The interchange should took place between Roy and Boy.","C":"Neither Roy nor Yoy has maroon after the interchange.","D":"The interchange should take place between Yoy and Moy."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"The interchange should took place between Roy and Boy.","solution_text":"Using Statement (B), The combinations can be (Roy - Blue, Boy - Yellow, Yoy – Red, Goy – Maroon, Moy – Green) or (Roy - Yellow, Boy - Red, Yoy – Blue, Goy – Maroon, Moy – Green). Statement (C) Does not provide any additional information. Using Statement (D) the condition that for exactly one friend the name and color have the same first letter.","question_operation":"identify_completing_statement","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]}]`);export{e as default};
